{"name":"Flat Aluminium","hex":"#c3c6cd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having no variations in height.","In a horizontal line or plane; not sloping."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["So as to be flat.","Completely, firmly, or unequivocally."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flat"},{"term":"aluminium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light, silvery metal extracted from bauxite, and a chemical element (symbol Al) with an atomic number of 13.\n\n An atom of this element.","An atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aluminium"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aluminium","blocks":[{"text":"Aluminium or aluminum is a chemical element; it has symbol Al and atomic number 13. It has a density lower than other common metals, about one-third that of steel. Aluminium has a great affinity toward oxygen, forming a protective layer of oxide on the surface when exposed to air. It visually resembles silver, both in its color and in its great ability to reflect light. It is soft, nonmagnetic, and ductile. It has one stable isotope, 27Al, which is highly abundant, making aluminium the 12th-most abundant element in the universe. The radioactivity of 26Al leads to it being used in radiometric dating."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Aluminium-4.jpg/330px-Aluminium-4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aluminium"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Lingot_aluminium.jpg/500px-Lingot_aluminium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Lingot_aluminium.jpg/500px-Lingot_aluminium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aluminium ingot","link":"https://en.wikipedi